      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by defining the general form of an arithmetic sequence. The nth term of an arithmetic sequence can be expressed as:\n\n\\[a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\\]\n\nwhere \\(a_n\\) is the nth term, \\(a_1\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number.\n\nGiven that the fifth term (\\(a_5\\)) is 11 and the common difference (\\(d\\)) is 1, we can substitute these values into the formula for the nth term:\n\n\\[a_5 = a_1 + (5-1)d\\]\n\\[11 = a_1 + 4 \\cdot 1\\]\n\\[11 = a_1 + 4\\]\n\nTo find \\(a_1\\), we subtract 4 from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[a_1 = 11 - 4\\]\n\\[a_1 = 7\\]\n\nNow that we have the first term \\(a_1 = 7\\), we can find the second term \\(a_2\\) using the formula for the nth term, with \\(n = 2\\):\n\n\\[a_2 = a_1 + (2-1)d\\]\n\\[a_2 = 7 + 1\\]\n\\[a_2 = 8\\]\n\nThe product of the first two terms is \\(a_1 \\times a_2\\):\n\n\\[a_1 \\times a_2 = 7 \\times 8\\]\n\\[a_1 \\times a_2 = 56\\]\n\nThus, the product of the first two terms is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
